Subject: [PATCH 23/44] docs: deep-idle research: m1n1's APSC snooze setup on
 T602x

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AuzUDcEeRJQ6RCdWW2XWLb
---
 docs/deep-idle-research.md | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/docs/deep-idle-research.md b/docs/deep-idle-research.md
index ffbf3af07453..fb6264c88fd0 100644
--- a/docs/deep-idle-research.md
+++ b/docs/deep-idle-research.md
@@ -647,6 +647,14 @@ tables.
   one. If the clock stays up, part of the "PD always" saving is leakage at a
   voltage nobody needs, and lowering the request when a cluster goes idle
   would add to it.
+  m1n1 points to the hardware doing it: for T602x it resets every cluster
+  to its "APSC" pstate (pstate 1) and sets "APSC Snooze" (cluster base +
+  0x200f8, bit 40; `src/cpufreq.c` `cpufreq_init_cluster()`), which reads
+  as an automatic drop to that pstate when the cluster idles. For T8103 and
+  T8112 it also copies the APSC pstate's table entry into a separate slot
+  (cluster + 0x70210, and + 0x7ffe8 on T8112); for T602x it does not, so
+  whether snooze has a valid target there is a question for the m1n1
+  authors, or for a read of those registers from m1n1's proxy.
 
 * Whether iBoot arms the wake path (reconfig engine, boot CPU RVBAR) for a
   raw image as it does for a kernelcache.
